The comment says this is for "Re-entry (a fault inside the dump)", but the flag is process-wide, not per-thread. Two problems follow:
- A worker that faults (or abort()s) while another thread is mid-dump calls _exit(3) immediately. That kills the process before the first thread writes its stack trace or `=== END CRASH ===`. The dump that survives is the one with no stack, and the corruption that caused the first fault often makes a second thread fault too.
- The check and the set are not atomic, so two threads can both pass the check and interleave their write(2) lines into the same O_SYNC fd.
The same code is in darwin/crash_handler_darwin.cpp:180-182 and android/crash_handler_android.cpp:294-296. The Windows CrashHandler has no guard at all and races the same way through ExitProcess(3).
Related gap: sigaltstack is installed only for the thread that ran CrashHandler_Install (:306-310). A worker thread's stack-overflow SIGSEGV therefore has no alternate stack and dies without a log.

