verdict.proof:
Checked on origin/main (<worktree>, android/app/src/main/java/org/openba/game/GameFilesJob.java). Not in the ledger: P0_MAP.md cites GameFilesJob only for zip-slip / .sav import (:275, :353), and the README and findings_*.json have no installStaged, renameTo or staging entry. No later commit fixes it.

The file comment at :18-19 promises: "the files are written into a folder beside WinData and moved into it only when every one of them arrived, so a cancel, a failure or a wrong folder leaves WinData as it was." The comment at :237-238 says "nothing is half-written in WinData." installStaged (:248-256) breaks that promise for any failure inside the move loop. For each staged file it deletes the WinData target (:250), then renames the staged file in (:253), and throws on the first failure. There is no rollback and no move-aside of the old set.

Both callers wrap it in try { ...; installStaged(...); } finally { GameFiles.deleteTree(staged); } (:347-365 folder, :434-507 archive). So after a throw at file k:
- files 1..k-1 in WinData are the new copies;
- file k is either deleted (delete succeeded at :250, renameTo failed at :253) or old (delete failed, so "cannot replace" is thrown);
- files k+1..n are still old;
- the new copies of k..n are deleted by the finally block.

Each step can be followed line by line. The concrete trigger is reachable: if WinData/<k-th name> is a non-empty directory, File.delete() returns false at :250 after earlier packages have already been replaced. A process kill between :250 and :253 leaves package k missing.

Practical impact is limited. renameTo within one directory on app-specific external storage rarely fails, and the source folder or zip is untouched, so retrying the copy restores a consistent set. That is why the severity is low. It is still a real defect against the documented contract.

